<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arverni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arverni were a Gallic people of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and the Roman period whose territory lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a>, the later Auvergne. One of the most powerful peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, they contested primacy with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, and under their chiefs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luernios">Luernius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> they dominated many neighbours until Bituitus was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 121 BCE. In 52 BCE the Arvernian chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> led the Gallic revolt against Caesar, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> before his defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> they formed one of the largest civitates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Augustonemetum</a>, the later Clermont-Ferrand, until their land was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 475.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arverni.jpg" alt="Arverni" width="330" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siren-Com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercingetorix_statère_MAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 58 and 50 BCE Julius Caesar conquered the rest of Gaul; Vercingetorix of the <b>Arverni</b> united most Gauls against him in 52 BCE and held Gergovia, but surrendered at Alesia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings still ruled elsewhere: Bituitus, king of the <b>Arverni</b>, led an army against Rome in 121 BCE and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
